n" [:option {:value "925768"} "1904"] "\n" [:option {:value "925784"} "1904"] "\n" [:option {:value "925882"} "1904"] "\n" [:option {:value "925922"} "1904"] "\n" [:option {:value "56225"} "1906"] "\n" [:option {:value "56461"} "1908"] "\n" [:option {:value "57196"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "57478"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "57760"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "58031"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "58303"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "58634"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "59008"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "59390"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "59789"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "60226"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "60678"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "61188"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "61763"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "62325"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "62818"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "63462"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "64137"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "64828"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "65488"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "66164"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "257298"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "302440"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "358704"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19000487"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2013644"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/75"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Pole Vault, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "10 – 11 July 1912"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/233"} "Stockholms Olympiastadion, Stockholm"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/56763"} "25 from 11 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "In this era, the United States absolutely dominated pole vaulting.  In fact, the final came down to 11 vaulters, eight of them American, with lone athletes from Canada, Germany, and Sweden.  There was no clear American favorite."] "\n\n" [:p {} "A review of recent performances shows how open the competition would be among the United States’ vaulters.  The most recent AAU titles had been won as follows:  1909 - Roy Paulding; 1910 - " [:a {:href "/athletes/78058"} "Harry Babcock"] "; and 1911 - a three-way tie between " [:a {:href "/athletes/78262"} "Ed Cook"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/78273"} "Frank Coyle"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/78099"} "Sam Bellah"] ".  Cook was the defending Olympic champion, having shared the " [:a {:href "/results/56461"} "1908"] " gold medal with " [:a {:href "/athletes/78453"} "A. C. Gilbert"] ".  The three U.S. Olympic trials went as follows:  Western - Bellah won; Central - " [:a {:href "/athletes/78827"} "Frank Murphy"] " won, with Coyle second; and Eastern - " [:a {:href "/athletes/79219"} "Marc Wright"] " won, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/78836"} "Frank Nelson"] " second."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The world record had also taken a recent beating by the American vault crew.  At the beginning of 1912, the mark stood at 3.93, set in Boulder, Colorado, on 27 May 1910, by Leland Scott.  However, at the IC4A title in Philadelphia on 1 June 1912, Yale’s Robert Gardner won, clearing 3.985 (13-1), the first 13-foot vault.  The mark lasted only a week, as Marc Wright won the eastern Olympic Trial with the first four-metre vault, clearing 13-2¼ (4.02)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Of the aforementioned American vaulters, all but Cook, Gilbert, Paulding, and Gardner competed at Stockholm, giving the United States six top vaulters from which to choose.  Among other nations, the Swedes championed " [:a {:href "/athletes/76432"} "Bertil Uggla"] ", while Canada sent " [:a {:href "/athletes/66080"} "William Halpenny"] ", who had been United States’ AAU champion in 1908, and would win the Canadian championship in 1911-1913."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final six vaulters came down to Uggla, Halpenny, and four Americans - Babcock, Nelson, Wright, and Murphy.  At 3.85 metres, Halpenny had to withdraw, having broken two ribs in clearing 3.80.  He was joined by Uggla and Murphy, who failed to clear 3.85.  At 3.85, Harold Babcock cleared on his first attempt, while Nelson and Wright needed two efforts.  But when the bar was raised to 3.95, only Babcock was successful, again on one attempt.  He took three shots at a new world record of 4.06 (13-3¾), but failed."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78058"} "Harry Babcock"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "3.65 (=1)"] [:td {} "3.95 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78836"} "Frank Nelson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "3.65 (=1)"] [:td {} "3.85 (=2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/79219"} "Marc Wright"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "3.65 (=1)"] [:td {} "3.85 (=2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", 